# Contents list

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

A contents list gives a small, predictable map of a related guide, so people can see where they are and where else they can go. It suits a short group of related help pages, with previous and next links at the end. Keep it to a handful of pages; a long list stops being a map.

The contents list is a Civica documentation pattern. Home Assistant has no contents list and it is not a dashboard card.

This page covers:

- Contents list
  - Civica Contents List Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A labelled `nav` landmark, so the list can be found and named.
- A short heading that names the scope: “On this page” or “In this guide”.
- A list of links. The current topic is plain text marked `aria-current="page"`.
- A thin rule down the left edge that separates the list from the page.

<h2 id="choose-the-navigation-scope">Choose the navigation scope</h2>

This page owns contents-navigation semantics. Choose the scope before writing links:

| Need | Label and target | Related guidance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sections of one article | “On this page”; stable heading fragments in reading order | [Publishing contents variant](../publishing/components/content-list.html) |
| Chapters of one short guide | “In this guide”; real page routes, with the current chapter identified | [Mini-hub composition](../patterns/mini-hub.html) |
| Independent tasks with saved progress | Task names and actual task status | [Complete multiple tasks](../patterns/complete-multiple-tasks.html) |

Do not mark every visible in-page fragment as the current page. For a multi-page guide, keep the contents order the same everywhere and pair it with [previous and next links](pagination.html) that name their destinations.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-contents-list-pattern"><code>Civica Contents List Pattern</code></h3>

Civica’s own markup: a `nav` with an `aria-label`, a heading and a list of links. It has a left rule and no other decoration, and it takes its text colour from the page.

- **In this guide.** Real page routes. The current page is text, not a link, and carries `aria-current="page"`.
- **On this page.** Links to headings on the same page. No item is marked current.

There are no Home Assistant theme variables for it, because it is not a Home Assistant element. In a dashboard, the nearest equivalent is a Markdown card with a short list of links to other views.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It works without scripts and needs no integration.
- A short list is quick to read and keeps the order of the guide.
- It is not a main menu, and it is not for a multi-page form. A form needs task progress and saved answers, not a list of pages.
- Links in a guide must go to real pages. A link that only moves within the example does not prove the destination exists.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

- Keep topic names short and the same as the page titles.
- Use the same order on every page of the guide.
- Name the scope in the heading so people know whether the links stay on this page.
- Keep the current topic distinguishable without colour. The current topic is plain text, not a link.
- Use `aria-current="page"` only when the item is the current route.
- In a Home Assistant help guide, keep topics like measurements, comparisons and missing readings in the same order, so people find them in the same place.

Success criteria that apply:

- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks and 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: a labelled `nav` and a real list.
- 2.4.4 Link Purpose, 2.4.5 Multiple Ways and 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: short, specific link text and a named scope.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the current topic is not shown by colour alone.
- 3.2.3 Consistent Navigation and 3.2.4 Consistent Identification: the same order and names on every page.
- 2.4.7 Focus Visible and 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): links keep a visible focus.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: the list works at 320 CSS pixels.

Test every destination, the browser’s Back button, long labels and keyboard use. Check it in light and dark modes, at 320 CSS pixels, and follow the [Civica accessibility checks](../accessibility/testing.html). See also [Navigation](navigation.html).

